Cryotherapy for Benign Skin Lesions (Warts/Sunspots)
General Practice Other
Typical benign hyperkeratotic lesion, Application of liquid nitrogen, Sharp stinging pain during freeze, Subsequent blister/eschar formation

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-010
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe pain and functional immobility secondary to a massively inflamed and traumatized plantar verruca.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient with a known plantar wart who experienced acute trauma to the lesion, resulting in severe, debilitating pain and an absolute inability to bear weight. Urgent face-to-face assessment was mandated to rule out deep soft tissue infection, manage the acute pain crisis, and perform immediate therapeutic cryotherapy to induce local necrosis and desensitize the exposed nerve endings. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have left the patient entirely immobile and in unmanageable pain overnight.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "I caught my foot on the stair edge, it ripped the top off the wart, and the pain is so sharp I cannot walk at all."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a direct visualization of a typical benign hyperkeratotic verruca on the plantar aspect of the right foot, present for three months. Two hours ago, the patient suffered blunt shear trauma to the lesion, partially avulsing the hyperkeratotic cap. This resulted in extreme, sharp pain and minor bleeding. There is an absence of atypical clinical features such as irregular borders, varied pigmentation, or recent rapid growth prior to the trauma. The pain is described as a severe, continuous burning that spikes to a 9/10 upon any weight-bearing attempt, rendering the patient completely bedbound.
Functional Status: Currently unable to ambulate or bear weight on the right foot due to severe localized pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Patient is unable to walk to the bathroom or kitchen safely; living alone.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, guarding the right foot, distressed when asked to stand.
Physical Exam: Integumentary: Examination of the right plantar metatarsal head reveals a 1cm typical benign hyperkeratotic lesion with a partially avulsed surface and pinpoint thrombosed capillaries visible. There is no spreading erythema, no purulent discharge, and no fluctuance. Palpation of the lesion elicits exquisite, sharp pain. The clinical pathway requires the application of liquid nitrogen, resulting in sharp stinging pain during the freeze, and anticipating subsequent blister and eschar formation. Expected investigation results include completely normal clinical healing without the need for histological examination assuming classic benign morphology.
Environmental Observations: Clear pathways, but patient cannot utilize them due to immobility.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Cryotherapy for Benign Skin Lesions (Warts/Sunspots) (Acutely Traumatized Plantar Verruca) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Retained Foreign Body - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear history of trauma to a pre-existing known wart, and dermoscopic visualization of typical thrombosed capillaries without any visible foreign material.

Plantar Fasciitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pain is strictly localized to the epidermal lesion itself, rather than the insertion point of the fascia at the heel, and was initiated by direct trauma.

Secondary Bacterial Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal temperature, absence of spreading warmth or redness beyond the immediate 1mm border of the lesion.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient presented with acute, severe localized pain and total inability to bear weight following mechanical trauma to a plantar verruca. Telephone triage accurately identified the functional immobility. Urgent face-to-face assessment was required to examine the wound, definitively rule out a secondary deep tissue infection or retained foreign body, and provide immediate therapeutic intervention. The application of liquid nitrogen (cryotherapy) was clinically indicated not only to treat the underlying viral lesion, but to immediately destroy the acutely exposed, sensitized nerve endings, providing rapid analgesia and restoring baseline mobility. Deferring this until the next in-hours period would have resulted in intractable pain and functional impairment overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Performed paring of the ragged hyperkeratotic edge. Executed cryotherapy via liquid nitrogen application (two freeze-thaw cycles of 10 seconds). Immediate formation of a localized white frost over the lesion was observed, with the patient reporting the expected sharp, stinging pain during freezing. Applied a protective hydrocolloid dressing to offload pressure.
Positive Behavioral Change: To facilitate healing and prevent further trauma, commit to wearing rigid-soled footwear (such as supportive sneakers) constantly whenever out of bed for the next week. The physical firmness of the shoe sole prevents the foot from flexing deeply and protects the forming blister from direct friction, reinforcing the behavior by immediately reducing pain during ambulation.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Hydrocolloid blister dressings (provided from bag).
Follow-up: Routine GP or podiatrist review in 14 days to assess eschar formation and eventual sloughing of the treated lesion.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 or attend the Emergency Department if the foot develops rapidly spreading red streaks, massive uncontrollable swelling, or if you develop a systemic fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Expect the development of local erythema and oedema within hours, followed by the formation of a serous or haemorrhagic blister over the next 24 to 48 hours. The pain will transition from a sharp sting to a dull ache.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek medical attention tomorrow if the pain becomes completely unresponsive to regular paracetamol and ibuprofen, or if yellow pus begins draining from under the dressing.
PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The freezing spray destroys the exposed nerve endings that were causing the severe sharp pain, while also killing the wart virus. A blister will form as part of the natural healing process.
Actionable Steps: Keep the hydrocolloid dressing on until it falls off naturally. Take paracetamol tonight for the aching. Do not walk barefoot under any circumstances this week.

